[Music]  [Applause]  in february  nasa's perseverance rover landed on mars  where the one-ton rover has begun its  search for past life  [Applause]  the purpose of the perseverance  expedition  on mars is to gather  signs of life that may have existed  there  early in the evolution of the planet  and if it can find any of those life  signs any bio signatures  pack them up cache them in very special  sterile tubes for retrieval  and return to earth nasa is currently  running detailed tests and calibrations  on the rover  during an initial evaluation period so  as we speak  they are still checking out all the  onboard engineering systems on the  perseverance rover it's a robotic arm  it's a camera mass they're testing out  the  heaters make sure it doesn't uh freeze  during the extremely cold martian nights  it will probably take another few weeks  to check out all these onboard  engineering systems  and then later this month they will  start looking around  jezreel crater and they are  seeking a nice flat  unobstructed site that could serve as  the first  helipad on mars the perseverance landing  marks nasa's fifth successful trip to  the red planet  its predecessors spirit opportunity and  sojourner all completed their missions  before losing contact  the curiosity rover which landed in 2012  is still operational  this is the first time we've ever gone  looking for something that we're going  to bring back to earth  that is really cool and one of the  reasons it's cool is because mars used  to be a lot like earth  and we don't understand yet why mars  doesn't look like earth anymore  jennifer trosper is the deputy project  manager at nasa's jet propulsion  laboratory in california better known as  jpl  she's been working on the perseverance  missions for eight years  i can tell you that perseverance is  operating  perfectly right now that all systems are  go  we're getting all kinds of data and lots  of images  i think the the amount of data  throughput we've had  has been phenomenal the um the early  images i think in terms of how much  we've been able to get down and just  look at  has been great and that whole data flow  has really kind of taxed our  infrastructure in a way that we're um  we're getting better at that but that  early on um caused us some snafus i felt  like the building was shaking there was  so much data coming in  it's like we just aren't used to this we  have to figure out how to manage it and  uh and so it's getting better but yeah  it's a lot of data to manage  last week the rover drove for the first  time on the martian landscape  and perseverance has already been back  over 8 000 photos capturing the craggy  terrain  and rocks and dunes the next thing we're  trying to do is get ready for the  helicopter mission in order to get ready  for the helicopter mission  we have to get the arm and the camera on  the arm  operational and so we'll be spending  about the next week doing that  i would say by by early april the  helicopter  early to mid april the helicopter should  be there ready to start its mission  i think you know kind of late summer is  when i  everything including the helicopter  mission is kind of  ready to go for going towards getting  those samples that we want to bring back  to earth those samples could help answer  one of the fundamental mysteries of the  universe  it's the culmination of 50 years  of exploration of mars and liftoff  as the countdown to mars continues this  is all designed to answer  a question that has really haunted  humankind  since we first started turning our eyes  skyward and looking at the stars  are we alone is there life  anywhere in the cosmos perseverance  if all goes as nasa hopes will offer  us the first concrete opportunity  to answer that question definitively

